The current `formChain` is a stub. I'll replace it with a backtracking search. It tries each stone, in both orientations, as the start of the chain. It extends the chain only where the next stone's left half matches the current right end, and accepts the chain once all stones are used and the first left half equals the last right half. Empty input returns an empty chain, and no valid arrangement throws `ChainNotFoundException`.

I can't see the `Domino` class, so I've assumed it exposes its two halves through `getValues()` returning a `List<Integer>`, and that it has a `reverse()` method. The accessors are isolated in two small helpers (`leftOf` and `rightOf`), so if your `Domino` uses different accessor names, only those helpers need changing. I've also assumed `ChainNotFoundException` has a `String` constructor.

src/main/java/Dominoes.java
```
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

class Dominoes {

    List<Domino> formChain(List<Domino> inputDominoes) throws ChainNotFoundException {
        if (inputDominoes == null || inputDominoes.isEmpty()) {
            return new ArrayList<>();
        }

        for (int i = 0; i < inputDominoes.size(); i++) {
            Domino first = inputDominoes.get(i);
            List<Domino> rest = new ArrayList<>(inputDominoes);
            rest.remove(i);

            for (Domino oriented : orientations(first)) {
                List<Domino> chain = new ArrayList<>();
                chain.add(oriented);
                if (extend(chain, rest)) {
                    return chain;
                }
            }
        }

        throw new ChainNotFoundException("No valid domino chain can be formed from the given stones.");
    }

    /**
     * Recursively tries to extend the chain with the remaining stones.
     * The chain is assumed to be non-empty.
     */
    private boolean extend(List<Domino> chain, List<Domino> remaining) {
        if (remaining.isEmpty()) {
            return leftOf(chain.get(0)) == rightOf(chain.get(chain.size() - 1));
        }

        int end = rightOf(chain.get(chain.size() - 1));

        for (int i = 0; i < remaining.size(); i++) {
            List<Domino> rest = new ArrayList<>(remaining);
            rest.remove(i);

            for (Domino oriented : orientations(remaining.get(i))) {
                if (leftOf(oriented) == end) {
                    chain.add(oriented);
                    if (extend(chain, rest)) {
                        return true;
                    }
                    chain.remove(chain.size() - 1);
                }
            }
        }

        return false;
    }

    private static List<Domino> orientations(Domino domino) {
        List<Domino> result = new ArrayList<>();
        result.add(domino);
        Domino reversed = domino.reverse();
        if (leftOf(reversed) != leftOf(domino) || rightOf(reversed) != rightOf(domino)) {
            result.add(reversed);
        }
        return result;
    }

    private static int leftOf(Domino domino) {
        return domino.getValues().get(0);
    }

    private static int rightOf(Domino domino) {
        return domino.getValues().get(1);
    }

}
```

I'll implement an Eulerian path/circuit search over the dominoes, treating each domino as an edge between its two values. A valid chain corresponds to an Eulerian circuit (since the first and last values must match). I use Hierholzer's algorithm-style backtracking via DFS: try each unused domino that connects to the current end, and backtrack if no full chain is found. This handles duplicate stones since each domino instance is tracked by index.

I assume `Domino` already exists with `getLeft()` and `getRight()` accessors (as used by the standard exercise), and `ChainNotFoundException` exists with a constructor taking a String.

