The skeleton throws in every export, so all five functions need real implementations. Only `promises.js` changes:

- `promisify` returns a wrapper that appends a Node-style `(error, data)` callback to the arguments. It rejects on error and resolves with `data` otherwise. It also forwards `this`, so promisified methods still work.
- `all` stores each result at its input index and resolves once every promise has fulfilled. An empty array resolves immediately with `[]`. The first rejection rejects the result.
- `allSettled` maps each promise to `{ status: 'fulfilled', value }` or `{ status: 'rejected', reason }` and passes them to `all`. The mapped promises never reject, so the result never rejects either.
- `race` settles with whichever input settles first.
- `any` resolves with the first fulfillment. If every promise rejects (including for an empty array), it rejects with an `AggregateError` containing the reasons in input order, matching the built-in `Promise.any`.

Non-promise inputs are normalized with `Promise.resolve`, as the built-ins do. The built-in `all`, `allSettled`, `race` and `any` are not used. I also removed the skeleton banner comment, since the file is no longer a skeleton.

