I'll implement all five functions in `promises.js`, without relying on the built-in `Promise` combinators.

- `promisify(fn)` returns a function that wraps the call in a new `Promise`, rejecting on a truthy first argument and resolving with the second otherwise. It also forwards `this` and any extra arguments.
- `all` tracks results by index, resolves once every promise has fulfilled (preserving order), and rejects on the first rejection.
- `allSettled` records `{status, value}` or `{status, reason}` for each input and resolves when all have settled.
- `race` settles with whichever input settles first.
- `any` resolves with the first fulfillment, and rejects with an `AggregateError` only after every input has rejected.

Inputs are wrapped with `Promise.resolve` so plain values also work. Note that `Promise.resolve` and `Promise` constructor are used only for creating and wrapping promises, not for the combinators being re-implemented.

