I'll implement the full doubly linked list in `src/lib.rs`, covering Steps 1–4 and the advanced Step 5 (covariance, Send/Sync). The `pre_implemented` module is referenced but not shown; I keep the `mod pre_implemented;` declaration and rely on it providing `push_*`/`pop_*` via the cursor methods, as described.

Design summary:
- `LinkedList<T>` holds raw `Option<NonNull<Node<T>>>` head/tail pointers and a length. Using `NonNull<Node<T>>` gives covariance over `T`.
- `Cursor` holds a `&mut LinkedList<T>` and an `Option<NonNull<Node<T>>>` current position (`None` = "ghost" position for empty lists / past-the-end).
- `Iter` holds shared pointers to the next node and remaining length.
- Invariants: every node pointed to by a live list is owned by that list; `prev`/`next` links are consistent; `len` equals the number of reachable nodes. Each unsafe block documents why it is sound.
- `Drop` frees all nodes.

Since the struct definitions must keep `PhantomData` usable for variance, I make `LinkedList` contain `PhantomData<Box<Node<T>>>`-free typing via `NonNull`, which is covariant and not `Send`/`Sync` automatically, so I implement those manually.

src/lib.rs
```
// this module adds some functionality based on the required implementations
// here like: `LinkedList::pop_back` or `Clone for LinkedList<T>`
// You are free to use anything in it, but it's mainly for the test framework.
mod pre_implemented;

use std::marker::PhantomData;
use std::ptr::NonNull;

struct Node<T> {
    element: T,
    prev: Option<NonNull<Node<T>>>,
    next: Option<NonNull<Node<T>>>,
}

pub struct LinkedList<T> {
    head: Option<NonNull<Node<T>>>,
    tail: Option<NonNull<Node<T>>>,
    len: usize,
    // Marks logical ownership of nodes of type T for drop check and variance.
    marker: PhantomData<T>,
}

pub struct Cursor<'a, T> {
    list: &'a mut LinkedList<T>,
    // `None` is the "ghost" position (before the front / after the back).
    current: Option<NonNull<Node<T>>>,
}

pub struct Iter<'a, T> {
    next: Option<NonNull<Node<T>>>,
    remaining: usize,
    marker: PhantomData<&'a T>,
}

// SAFETY: The list owns its nodes exclusively, so it can be sent to another
// thread whenever `T` can be sent.
unsafe impl<T: Send> Send for LinkedList<T> {}
// SAFETY: Shared references to the list only allow shared access to the
// elements, so sharing is sound whenever `T` is `Sync`.
unsafe impl<T: Sync> Sync for LinkedList<T> {}

// SAFETY: Same reasoning as for `LinkedList`; the cursor gives mutable access
// to the list and its elements, so `T: Send` is required.
unsafe impl<T: Send> Send for Cursor<'_, T> {}
unsafe impl<T: Sync> Sync for Cursor<'_, T> {}

// SAFETY: The iterator only yields shared references to elements.
unsafe impl<T: Sync> Send for Iter<'_, T> {}
unsafe impl<T: Sync> Sync for Iter<'_, T> {}

impl<T> Default for LinkedList<T> {
    fn default() -> Self {
        Self::new()
    }
}

impl<T> LinkedList<T> {
    pub fn new() -> Self {
        LinkedList {
            head: None,
            tail: None,
            len: 0,
            marker: PhantomData,
        }
    }

    // You may be wondering why it's necessary to have is_empty()
    // when it can easily be determined from len().
    // It's good custom to have both because len() can be expensive for some types,
    // whereas is_empty() is almost always cheap.
    // (Also ask yourself whether len() is expensive for LinkedList)
    pub fn is_empty(&self) -> bool {
        self.head.is_none()
    }

    pub fn len(&self) -> usize {
        self.len
    }

    /// Return a cursor positioned on the front element
    pub fn cursor_front(&mut self) -> Cursor<'_, T> {
        let current = self.head;
        Cursor {
            list: self,
            current,
        }
    }

    /// Return a cursor positioned on the back element
    pub fn cursor_back(&mut self) -> Cursor<'_, T> {
        let current = self.tail;
        Cursor {
            list: self,
            current,
        }
    }

    /// Return an iterator that moves from front to back
    pub fn iter(&self) -> Iter<'_, T> {
        Iter {
            next: self.head,
            remaining: self.len,
            marker: PhantomData,
        }
    }
}

// the cursor is expected to act as if it is at the position of an element
// and it also has to work with and be able to insert into an empty list.
impl<T> Cursor<'_, T> {
    /// Take a mutable reference to the current element
    pub fn peek_mut(&mut self) -> Option<&mut T> {
        // SAFETY: `current` is either `None` or points to a live node owned by
        // the list. We hold `&mut` to the list, so no other reference to the
        // node exists while the returned borrow lives (tied to `&mut self`).
        unsafe { self.current.map(|mut n| &mut n.as_mut().element) }
    }

    /// Move one position forward (towards the back) and
    /// return a reference to the new position
    #[allow(clippy::should_implement_trait)]
    pub fn next(&mut self) -> Option<&mut T> {
        // SAFETY: `current` is a live node or `None`; its `next` link is
        // consistent with the list structure, so the target is live too.
        unsafe {
            self.current = match self.current {
                Some(node) => node.as_ref().next,
                None => self.list.head,
            };
        }
        self.peek_mut()
    }

    /// Move one position backward (towards the front) and
    /// return a reference to the new position
    pub fn prev(&mut self) -> Option<&mut T> {
        // SAFETY: as in `next`, links are consistent and nodes are live.
        unsafe {
            self.current = match self.current {
                Some(node) => node.as_ref().prev,
                None => self.list.tail,
            };
        }
        self.peek_mut()
    }

    /// Remove and return the element at the current position and move the cursor
    /// to the neighboring element that's closest to the back. This can be
    /// either the next or previous position.
    pub fn take(&mut self) -> Option<T> {
        let node = self.current?;
        // SAFETY: `node` is a live node owned by the list. We unlink it, fix the
        // neighbours' links and head/tail, then reclaim the Box exactly once.
        unsafe {
            let boxed = Box::from_raw(node.as_ptr());
            let prev = boxed.prev;
            let next = boxed.next;

            match prev {
                Some(mut p) => p.as_mut().next = next,
                None => self.list.head = next,
            }
            match next {
                Some(mut n) => n.as_mut().prev = prev,
                None => self.list.tail = prev,
            }
            self.list.len -= 1;

            // Move to the neighbour closest to the back.
            self.current = next.or(prev);

            Some(boxed.element)
        }
    }

    pub fn insert_after(&mut self, element: T) {
        let new = Box::into_raw(Box::new(Node {
            element,
            prev: None,
            next: None,
        }));
        // SAFETY: `new` is a freshly allocated, uniquely owned node. Linking it
        // requires updating only live nodes/list pointers, keeping invariants.
        unsafe {
            let mut new_nn = NonNull::new_unchecked(new);
            match self.current {
                Some(mut cur) => {
                    let next = cur.as_ref().next;
                    new_nn.as_mut().prev = Some(cur);
                    new_nn.as_mut().next = next;
                    cur.as_mut().next = Some(new_nn);
                    match next {
                        Some(mut n) => n.as_mut().prev = Some(new_nn),
                        None => self.list.tail = Some(new_nn),
                    }
                }
                None => {
                    // Ghost position: insert at the front.
                    let old_head = self.list.head;
                    new_nn.as_mut().next = old_head;
                    match old_head {
                        Some(mut h) => h.as_mut().prev = Some(new_nn),
                        None => self.list.tail = Some(new_nn),
                    }
                    self.list.head = Some(new_nn);
                }
            }
            self.list.len += 1;
        }
    }

    pub fn insert_before(&mut self, element: T) {
        let new = Box::into_raw(Box::new(Node {
            element,
            prev: None,
            next: None,
        }));
        // SAFETY: as in `insert_after`.
        unsafe {
            let mut new_nn = NonNull::new_unchecked(new);
            match self.current {
                Some(mut cur) => {
                    let prev = cur.as_ref().prev;
                    new_nn.as_mut().next = Some(cur);
                    new_nn.as_mut().prev = prev;
                    cur.as_mut().prev = Some(new_nn);
                    match prev {
                        Some(mut p) => p.as_mut().next = Some(new_nn),
                        None => self.list.head = Some(new_nn),
                    }
                }
                None => {
                    // Ghost position: insert at the back.
                    let old_tail = self.list.tail;
                    new_nn.as_mut().prev = old_tail;
                    match old_tail {
                        Some(mut t) => t.as_mut().next = Some(new_nn),
                        None => self.list.head = Some(new_nn),
                    }
                    self.list.tail = Some(new_nn);
                }
            }
            self.list.len += 1;
        }
    }
}

impl<T> Drop for LinkedList<T> {
    fn drop(&mut self) {
        let mut cur = self.head;
        while let Some(node) = cur {
            // SAFETY: each node was allocated with Box::into_raw and is freed
            // exactly once here; we read `next` before freeing it.
            unsafe {
                let boxed = Box::from_raw(node.as_ptr());
                cur = boxed.next;
            }
        }
        self.head = None;
        self.tail = None;
        self.len = 0;
    }
}

impl<'a, T> Iterator for Iter<'a, T> {
    type Item = &'a T;

    fn next(&mut self) -> Option<&'a T> {
        match self.next {
            Some(node) if self.remaining > 0 => {
                // SAFETY: the iterator borrows the list for `'a`, so the node is
                // live and is not mutated while we hand out shared references.
                unsafe {
                    let r = node.as_ref();
                    self.next = r.next;
                    self.remaining -= 1;
                    Some(&r.element)
                }
            }
            _ => None,
        }
    }
}
