I'll implement the `graph` module as an internal DSL-style builder: a `Graph` with attributes (`graph [..]`), nodes with attributes, and edges with attributes, using the builder pattern. I'll keep `Graph::new()` and add builder methods that chain.

Design:
- `Graph` holds `attrs: HashMap<String, String>`, `nodes: Vec<Node>`, `edges: Vec<Edge>`.
- `Node` holds `name` and `attrs`.
- `Edge` holds `from`, `to`, and `attrs`.
- `Graph::with_attr`, `Graph::with_node`, `Graph::with_edge` take builder-style `self` and return `Self`.
- `Graph::node(name)` and `Graph::edge(from, to)` return builders for attaching attributes via `.with_attr`, then `.build()` back to the graph. To keep it simple and consistent, I'll use `NodeBuilder`/`EdgeBuilder` that hold a `Graph` and finish with `.end()`.

src/lib.rs
```
pub mod graph {
    use std::collections::HashMap;

    #[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Eq)]
    pub struct Node {
        name: String,
        attrs: HashMap<String, String>,
    }

    impl Node {
        pub fn new(name: &str) -> Self {
            Node {
                name: name.to_string(),
                attrs: HashMap::new(),
            }
        }

        pub fn with_attr(mut self, key: &str, value: &str) -> Self {
            self.attrs.insert(key.to_string(), value.to_string());
            self
        }

        pub fn name(&self) -> &str {
            &self.name
        }

        pub fn attr(&self, key: &str) -> Option<&str> {
            self.attrs.get(key).map(|s| s.as_str())
        }

        pub fn attrs(&self) -> &HashMap<String, String> {
            &self.attrs
        }
    }

    #[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Eq)]
    pub struct Edge {
        from: String,
        to: String,
        attrs: HashMap<String, String>,
    }

    impl Edge {
        pub fn new(from: &str, to: &str) -> Self {
            Edge {
                from: from.to_string(),
                to: to.to_string(),
                attrs: HashMap::new(),
            }
        }

        pub fn with_attr(mut self, key: &str, value: &str) -> Self {
            self.attrs.insert(key.to_string(), value.to_string());
            self
        }

        pub fn from(&self) -> &str {
            &self.from
        }

        pub fn to(&self) -> &str {
            &self.to
        }

        pub fn attr(&self, key: &str) -> Option<&str> {
            self.attrs.get(key).map(|s| s.as_str())
        }

        pub fn attrs(&self) -> &HashMap<String, String> {
            &self.attrs
        }
    }

    #[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Eq)]
    pub struct Graph {
        attrs: HashMap<String, String>,
        nodes: Vec<Node>,
        edges: Vec<Edge>,
    }

    impl Default for Graph {
        fn default() -> Self {
            Self::new()
        }
    }

    impl Graph {
        pub fn new() -> Self {
            Graph {
                attrs: HashMap::new(),
                nodes: Vec::new(),
                edges: Vec::new(),
            }
        }

        /// Sets a graph-level attribute, e.g. `graph [bgcolor="yellow"]`.
        pub fn with_attr(mut self, key: &str, value: &str) -> Self {
            self.attrs.insert(key.to_string(), value.to_string());
            self
        }

        /// Adds a node with no attributes.
        pub fn with_node(self, name: &str) -> Self {
            self.with_node_built(Node::new(name))
        }

        /// Adds a fully built node.
        pub fn with_node_built(mut self, node: Node) -> Self {
            self.nodes.push(node);
            self
        }

        /// Adds an edge with no attributes.
        pub fn with_edge(self, from: &str, to: &str) -> Self {
            self.with_edge_built(Edge::new(from, to))
        }

        /// Adds a fully built edge.
        pub fn with_edge_built(mut self, edge: Edge) -> Self {
            self.edges.push(edge);
            self
        }

        pub fn attr(&self, key: &str) -> Option<&str> {
            self.attrs.get(key).map(|s| s.as_str())
        }

        pub fn attrs(&self) -> &HashMap<String, String> {
            &self.attrs
        }

        pub fn nodes(&self) -> &[Node] {
            &self.nodes
        }

        pub fn edges(&self) -> &[Edge] {
            &self.edges
        }

        pub fn node(&self, name: &str) -> Option<&Node> {
            self.nodes.iter().find(|n| n.name() == name)
        }
    }
}
