I'll implement an Eulerian path/circuit search on the domino graph: each domino is an edge between its two values, and we need an Eulerian circuit using all edges (which guarantees the first and last values match). I'll use a recursive backtracking search over the dominoes, which handles duplicates naturally, and throw `ChainNotFoundException` when no valid chain exists.

Note: `Domino` and `ChainNotFoundException` are assumed to exist in the project (as referenced by the stub signature). The `Domino` class is expected to expose `getLeft()` and `getRight()` accessors, and `ChainNotFoundException` is expected to have a `String` constructor. Since I don't have those files, I'll rely on the standard exercism API: `Domino(int, int)`, `getLeft()`, `getRight()`, and `reverse()`.

Approach:
- Empty input returns an empty chain.
- Try each remaining domino as the starting stone, in either orientation.
- Recursively extend the chain by matching the tail value with a remaining domino's left side (flipping as needed).
- When all dominoes are used, check the chain closes (first left == last right).

src/main/java/Dominoes.java
```
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

class Dominoes {

    List<Domino> formChain(List<Domino> inputDominoes) throws ChainNotFoundException {
        if (inputDominoes == null || inputDominoes.isEmpty()) {
            return new ArrayList<>();
        }

        List<Domino> remaining = new ArrayList<>(inputDominoes);
        List<Domino> chain = new ArrayList<>();

        // Try each domino as the starting stone (in both orientations)
        for (int i = 0; i < remaining.size(); i++) {
            Domino first = remaining.remove(i);

            chain.add(first);
            if (search(chain, remaining)) {
                return chain;
            }
            chain.clear();

            // Try the reversed orientation of the starting stone
            chain.add(reversed(first));
            if (search(chain, remaining)) {
                return chain;
            }
            chain.clear();

            remaining.add(i, first);
        }

        throw new ChainNotFoundException("No valid domino chain exists for the given dominoes");
    }

    private boolean search(List<Domino> chain, List<Domino> remaining) {
        if (remaining.isEmpty()) {
            // Chain is closed if the first left matches the last right
            return chain.get(0).getLeft() == chain.get(chain.size() - 1).getRight();
        }

        int tail = chain.get(chain.size() - 1).getRight();

        for (int i = 0; i < remaining.size(); i++) {
            Domino candidate = remaining.get(i);

            if (candidate.getLeft() == tail) {
                remaining.remove(i);
                chain.add(candidate);
                if (search(chain, remaining)) {
                    return true;
                }
                chain.remove(chain.size() - 1);
                remaining.add(i, candidate);
            } else if (candidate.getRight() == tail) {
                Domino flipped = reversed(candidate);
                remaining.remove(i);
                chain.add(flipped);
                if (search(chain, remaining)) {
                    return true;
                }
                chain.remove(chain.size() - 1);
                remaining.add(i, candidate);
            }
        }

        return false;
    }

    private Domino reversed(Domino domino) {
        return new Domino(domino.getRight(), domino.getLeft());
    }

}
```

I'll implement an Eulerian path/circuit search: treat each domino as an edge between its two values, and find a circuit using all edges (Hierholzer's algorithm), starting from any vertex. Since the chain must have matching ends, we need an Eulerian circuit, which exists iff every vertex has even degree and all edges are connected. If no such chain exists, we throw `ChainNotFoundException`.

I'm assuming `Domino` exposes `getLeft()`/`getRight()` or similar; to stay self-contained, I'll only rely on the existing `Domino` class's constructor `new Domino(int, int)` and accessor methods. Since the `Domino` class isn't shown, I'll use the commonly provided accessors `getValues()` returning a `List<Integer>` of two values, which is the standard exercism stub definition.

